    "ex-two-factor-interaction-and-scale-dependence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-two-level-full-factorial-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an integer $k\\ge1$ and write $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$. A **two-level full factorial design** has run set\n$$\\mathcal X_k=\\{-1,+1\\}^{[k]},$$\nso every one of the $2^k$ sign combinations occurs once. For a run $x\\in\\mathcal X_k$ and a subset $A\\subseteq[k]$, define its sign column by\n$$x_A=\\prod_{j\\in A}x_j,$$\nwhere the empty product is $x_\\varnothing=1$. The design matrix $X$ has one row for each run, one column for each subset of factors, and entry $X_{x,A}=x_A$. Any fixed ordering of the runs and subsets may be used.\n\nThe run set and subset index set both have $2^k$ elements: a subset $A$ corresponds to the sign vector whose $j$th coordinate is $+1$ exactly when $j\\in A$. Thus $X$ is a square $2^k\\times2^k$ matrix.\n\nFor a real response table $y=(y_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}$, define its normalized factorial coefficient by\n$$\\widehat\\beta_A(y)=2^{-k}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X_k}x_Ay_x.$$\nFor nonempty $A$, put $G_{A,+}=\\{x:x_A=+1\\}$ and $G_{A,-}=\\{x:x_A=-1\\}$. Both sets are nonempty: the all-positive run lies in $G_{A,+}$, and flipping any coordinate in $A$ gives a run in $G_{A,-}$. Define the factorial effect on this response scale as\n$$\\widehat\\tau_A(y)=\\frac{1}{|G_{A,+}|}\\sum_{x\\in G_{A,+}}y_x-\\frac{1}{|G_{A,-}|}\\sum_{x\\in G_{A,-}}y_x.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Here $x_{\\{1,2\\}}=x_1x_2$ is $+1$ on runs $--$ and $++$, and $-1$ on runs $+-$ and $-+$. By [F1], the raw-scale $AB$ effect is $\\frac{1+6}{2}-\\frac{2+3}{2}=1$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The log-transformed responses remain indexed by these same four runs, so [F1] uses the same two sign groups. The log-scale $AB$ effect is $\\frac{\\log 1+\\log 6}{2}-\\frac{\\log 2+\\log 3}{2}=0$, using $\\log 1=0$ and $\\log 6=\\log 2+\\log 3$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The raw-scale interaction effect is nonzero while the log-scale interaction effect is zero, so this table's interaction depends on the declared response scale. The run set alone specifies no response additivity or physical mechanism.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example has two factors and four runs."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 computes the log-scale interaction contrast to be zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example uses a two-factor design."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–2.1 exhibit a nonzero raw-scale effect and a zero log-scale effect."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval-valued parameter is present."
        },
        {
